<section class="content">

<h3>Notes for the Catechist</h3>

<h4>The story of Saint Augustine</h4>
<p>It is a legend, not a historical fact, but it serves as a hook. The point is not the impossibility of understanding God, but that this incomprehensible God chose to create out of love. Make sure the group does not stay at &laquo;God is incomprehensible&raquo; but moves toward &laquo;God is love that creates.&raquo;</p>

<h4>Creatio ex nihilo</h4>
<p>This concept is fundamental. In the ancient world, many religions believed in eternal matter. Biblical revelation is unique: God does not battle chaos, he dominates it with his word. He does not shape eternal clay; he calls into existence what did not exist. It is important for the group to understand that this is not a scientific theory but a theological truth: the universe depends totally on God.</p>

<h4>The goodness of creation</h4>
<p>Many people, even Christians, have a negative view of the body and matter. It is important to emphasize that God declared his creation &laquo;very good&raquo; (Gen 1:31). Matter is not evil; it is God&rsquo;s gift. This opposes both materialism (which reduces everything to matter) and dualism (which despises matter).</p>

<h4>The problem of evil</h4>
<p>It is almost certain that someone will ask about suffering. Do not offer easy answers. The essential point: God does not will evil, he permits it while respecting freedom, and from every evil he brings a greater good. The cross is the definitive proof. You can cite Saint Thomas: &laquo;God permits evil in order to bring from it a greater good.&raquo; It is not about justifying suffering, but about trusting that God does not lose control.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Age Adaptation</h3>

<h4>🌱 Seed (ages 7-12)</h4>
<p>Use the image of a painter who paints a picture for pleasure, not because someone paid him. Activities: draw something from creation and write &laquo;God wanted this to exist,&raquo; color the days of creation, memorize Wis 11:24 with gestures.</p>

<h4>🌿 Sprouts (ages 13-17)</h4>
<p>Connect with the question of evil: why is there suffering if God is good? Do not dodge the question. Use the film &laquo;The Tree of Life&raquo; by Malick as a reference. Providence is not a remote control; it is a presence that accompanies. The cross transforms evil, it does not cancel it.</p>

<h4>🪴 Root (adults new to the faith)</h4>
<p>Many adults have suffered injustice and find it hard to trust in providence. Be sensitive: do not minimize pain. Help them see that faith is not an explanation of evil, but a trust in the midst of evil. God is not absent; he is present even when we do not feel him. The example of Job can help.</p>

<h4>🌳 Tree (formed adults)</h4>
<p>Go deeper into the doctrine of providence: God causes everything in a primary way, creatures in a secondary way (CCC 308). Human freedom does not compete with providence but cooperates with it. You can cite Saint Augustine and Saint Thomas on the relationship between freedom and providence.</p>

</section>

